\description{
\code{apa_tidy()} turns a fitted model, a set of posterior draws or a
comparison object into an \link{apabayes_tidy} table: fixed column names,
the numbers computed by easystats, no formatting. It is the whole of
the extract layer; everything apabayes reports is built from its
output.
}
\section{Methods (by class)}{
\itemize{
\item \code{apa_tidy(BFBayesFactor)}: A \code{BFBayesFactor} object from BayesFactor
(\code{\link[BayesFactor:anovaBF]{BayesFactor::anovaBF()}}, \code{\link[BayesFactor:lmBF]{BayesFactor::lmBF()}},
\code{\link[BayesFactor:ttestBF]{BayesFactor::ttestBF()}}, \code{\link[BayesFactor:correlationBF]{BayesFactor::correlationBF()}}, ...), as a
\code{bf_models} table read from the object: the denominator model first,
then every numerator in the object's order, each named by
BayesFactor's short name (\code{model}, made unique as BayesFactor names
its rows: \code{"wt"}, \code{"wt #1"}) and long name (\code{name}). \code{log_bf}
is BayesFactor's own natural-log Bayes factor, \code{bf} its exponential,
\code{error} its proportional numerical error (\code{NA} on the denominator row
and where BayesFactor records none, 0 for the exact families), and
\code{method} the prior family (\code{"JZS (BayesFactor)"},
\code{"Jeffreys-beta* (BayesFactor)"}, ...). \code{post_prob} is computed at
equal prior odds, as on the \code{bayesfactor_models} route. A numerator
identical to the denominator (\code{bf / bf[1]}) is not repeated. A
\code{BFBayesFactorList} (\code{bf / bf}) holds several denominators and is
refused; report one column of it, \code{x[, j]}. Parameter estimates come
from the draws: \code{apa_tidy(BayesFactor::posterior(bf, iterations = ))}
reads a \code{BFmcmc} object through the draws route, and takes that
route's arguments.

\item \code{apa_tidy(BFBayesFactorList)}: A \code{BFBayesFactorList} is refused: it holds Bayes
factors against several denominators, and a table has one.

\item \code{apa_tidy(BFmcmc)}: A \code{BFmcmc} object from \code{\link[BayesFactor:posterior]{BayesFactor::posterior()}}
is read by the draws route, with every argument of that route. An
object with several numerators needs \verb{index =} in
\code{BayesFactor::posterior()}.

\item \code{apa_tidy(bayesfactor_inclusion)}: The output of \code{\link[bayestestR:bayesfactor_inclusion]{bayestestR::bayesfactor_inclusion()}},
as a \code{bf_inclusion} table: one row per \code{term}, with the prior and
posterior inclusion probabilities (\code{p_prior}, \code{p_posterior}), the
inclusion Bayes factor \code{bf} and its log \code{log_bf}. A term in every
model has no inclusion Bayes factor (\code{NA}, where bayestestR has
\code{NaN}); a posterior inclusion probability that rounds to 1 gives an
infinite one, which is kept. The \code{averaging} attribute says whether
the models were averaged over \code{"all"} or \code{"matched"} models,
\code{prior_odds} whether the prior odds were \code{"equal"} or \code{"custom"} (the
values in \code{prior_odds_values}). bayestestR does not record how the
models' Bayes factors were computed, so \code{bf_method} is \code{NA}.

\item \code{apa_tidy(blavaan)}: A \code{blavaan} fit from \code{\link[blavaan:bcfa]{blavaan::bcfa()}},
\code{\link[blavaan:bsem]{blavaan::bsem()}} or \code{\link[blavaan:bgrowth]{blavaan::bgrowth()}}. Estimates, the credible
interval and pd come from \code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}}, which
reports the \emph{free} parameters only — the fixed marker loading is
absent, and \code{p} is \code{NA} throughout. \code{term} is blavaan's own
parameter name (\code{visual=~x1}, \code{x1~~x1}) and \code{label} the same with
spaces. Here \code{component} is \code{"all"}, \code{"latent"} or \code{"residual"},
blavaan's own vocabulary rather than lavaan's six names.

R-hat and both ESS columns come from
\code{\link[posterior:summarise_draws]{posterior::summarise_draws()}} over \code{blavaan::blavInspect(x, "mcmc")}, and blavaan's own are never computed. They therefore
\strong{differ from what \code{blavaan::summary()} prints}, which reports
\code{blavInspect(x, "rhat")} and \code{"neff"}: one ESS where the contract
has two, from an estimator that is neither the bulk nor the tail ESS
the "greater than 400" rule of thumb is defined for.

\code{standardize} reads \code{\link[blavaan:standardizedPosterior]{blavaan::standardizedPosterior()}} instead of
easystats, which cannot standardize a blavaan fit at all. The
standardized solution covers the whole parameter table, so its rows
are a \emph{superset} of the unstandardized ones: the fixed markers
appear, with a real interval, and under \code{"std.all"} the latent
variances are exactly 1. Those rows carry no component information,
so \code{component} is \code{NA} there and cannot be combined with
\code{standardize}; select rows with \code{variables} instead. A multi-group
fit is refused.

\item \code{apa_tidy(brmsfit)}: A \code{brmsfit}, and by inheritance a \code{bmmfit}.
Estimates, interval, pd and the ROPE percentage come from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}}, R-hat and both ESS columns from
\code{\link[bayestestR:diagnostic_posterior]{bayestestR::diagnostic_posterior()}}; apabayes computes no summary
of its own. A bmm model parameter is a brms distributional
parameter, so \code{component} carries it (\code{drift}, \code{kappa}, ...).

One shape is out of reach upstream: a model whose response is a
matrix with a \code{trials()} term (\code{family = multinomial()}, which is
every bmm M3 fit) fails inside \code{insight::get_data()} on R 4.3 or
newer, before any number is computed. The method then aborts
naming \code{apa_tidy(brms::as_draws_df(fit))}, which reads the draws
directly; \code{\link[=apa_tidy_diagnostics]{apa_tidy_diagnostics()}} and \code{\link[=apa_convergence]{apa_convergence()}} do not go
through easystats and are unaffected.

\item \code{apa_tidy(brmshypothesis)}: The output of \code{\link[brms:hypothesis]{brms::hypothesis()}}. Estimates,
interval, evidence ratio and posterior probability are brms's own;
apabayes adds \code{bf10} (\code{1 / evid_ratio} for a point hypothesis, the
evidence ratio itself for a directional one) and \code{directional}.
easystats has no method for this class, so no easystats function is
called, and \code{package_versions} credits \code{brms} alone.

\item \code{apa_tidy(compare.loo)}: The output of \code{\link[loo:loo_compare]{loo::loo_compare()}}, of either
shape: the data frame loo 2.10.0 and later return, or the matrix of
earlier versions. One row per model, best first, with loo's own
numbers: the ELPD difference to the best model and its standard
error, the model's ELPD, \code{p_loo} and LOOIC. No loo function is
called; \code{loo} must be installed to record its version. apabayes
never turns an ELPD difference into a Bayes factor or a weight into
evidence (ARCHITECTURE decision 17).

\item \code{apa_tidy(bayesfactor_models)}: The output of \code{\link[bayestestR:bayesfactor_models]{bayestestR::bayesfactor_models()}}.
\code{bf} is \code{exp(log_BF)}, kept next to \code{log_bf} because the exponential
overflows above a log Bayes factor of about 709; \code{denominator} marks
the row every Bayes factor is taken against; \code{method} is how they
were computed (bridge sampling, the BIC approximation, BayesFactor's
JZS). \code{post_prob} is the posterior probability of each model under
\strong{equal prior odds}, the assumption the \code{prior_odds} attribute
records. \code{model} is the model as bayestestR names it (the formula's
right-hand side for most classes) and the extra column \code{name} the
argument it was passed as.

\item \code{apa_tidy(emmGrid)}: An \code{emmGrid} from \code{\link[emmeans:emmeans]{emmeans::emmeans()}} or
\code{\link[emmeans:contrast]{emmeans::contrast()}} on a Bayesian fit: one row per grid row, the
contrast string or, on a grid of marginal means, the row's label as
emmeans writes it (\code{cyl_f4}, \verb{cyl_f4 auto}), in the \code{contrast}
column. The estimate, interval, pd and ROPE share come from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}}. \code{ci = "hdi"}, the default, is the
highest-density interval \code{summary(emmGrid)} prints (emmeans calls it
HPD; the numbers are identical) and is labelled HDI; \code{ci = "eti"}
gives the equal-tailed interval. A \code{by}
variable's values go into the \code{group} column and its name into the
\code{by} attribute; every grid variable is kept as an extra column. The
grid is reported as it is: subset it with \code{[} first to report fewer
rows. A grid without posterior draws (from a frequentist fit) is
refused, and so is an \code{emm_list} (\code{emmeans(fit, pairwise ~ f)}),
which holds two tables of different kinds — pass one of its parts.

\item \code{apa_tidy(emm_list)}: An \code{emm_list}, which \code{\link[emmeans:emmeans]{emmeans::emmeans()}} returns
for a two-sided formula (\code{pairwise ~ f}), is refused: it holds a
table of means and a table of contrasts, and one tidy table reports
one kind. Pass \code{x$emmeans} or \code{x$contrasts}.

\item \code{apa_tidy(easycorrelation)}: A table of Bayesian correlations from
\code{\link[correlation:correlation]{correlation::correlation()}} or \code{\link[correlation:cor_test]{correlation::cor_test()}} with
\code{bayesian = TRUE}, as a \code{correlations} table: one row per pair,
named \code{var1~~var2}, with the posterior estimate of the correlation,
its interval, pd, the ROPE share, the Bayes factor and the pairwise
n, all read from the table. The table does not record which interval
or which centrality it holds, so \code{ci} and \code{centrality} must name what
it was computed with; they default to correlation's own defaults
(\code{bayesian_ci_method = "hdi"}, the median) and apabayes cannot check
them. A table computed with \code{centrality = "all"} has no \code{rho} column
and is read from the column \code{centrality} names. correlation writes
its \code{ci} argument into the table without applying it (the bounds are
95 \% whatever \code{ci} was), so a table labelled with any other level is
refused. The ROPE share is reported without bounds, which the table
does not carry. Frequentist tables and tables with diagonal rows
(\code{redundant = TRUE}) are refused.

\item \code{apa_tidy(default)}: Anything \code{\link[posterior:as_draws_df]{posterior::as_draws_df()}} accepts —
\code{stanfit}, \code{CmdStanFit}, \code{mcmc}, \code{mcmc.list}, a draws matrix or a
data frame of draws — is converted and handed to the \code{draws}
method. A data frame, tibble (grouped or not) or data.table counts as
draws only when its columns are all numeric; a summary table (one
with a class of its own, or with a label column) is refused rather
than read as draws. The class of the object you passed is kept as
the \code{source_class} attribute.

\item \code{apa_tidy(runjags)}: A \code{runjags} object keeps its chains in \verb{$mcmc};
\code{posterior} has no method for the object itself.

\item \code{apa_tidy(draws)}: Posterior draws. Estimates, interval, pd and the
ROPE percentage come from \code{\link[bayestestR:describe_posterior]{bayestestR::describe_posterior()}}, R-hat
and ESS from \code{\link[posterior:summarise_draws]{posterior::summarise_draws()}}; apabayes computes no
summary of its own.

\item \code{apa_tidy(lavaan)}: A \code{lavaan} fit from \code{\link[lavaan:cfa]{lavaan::cfa()}},
\code{\link[lavaan:sem]{lavaan::sem()}} or \code{\link[lavaan:growth]{lavaan::growth()}}. Estimates, standard errors,
the confidence interval and the p value come from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}}, which reads
\code{\link[lavaan:parameterEstimates]{lavaan::parameterEstimates()}} or, with \code{standardize},
\code{\link[lavaan:standardizedSolution]{lavaan::standardizedSolution()}}. \code{term} is lavaan's own parameter
name (\code{visual=~x1}, \code{x1~~x1}, \code{dem60~ind60}, \code{x1~1}; \code{.g2} appended
in the second group of a multi-group fit) and \code{label} the same with
spaces. Here \code{component} is \code{"all"} or one or more of \code{"loading"},
\code{"regression"}, \code{"correlation"}, \code{"variance"}, \code{"mean"} and
\code{"defined"}. \code{centrality} is \code{NA} on the result — the estimate is a
maximum-likelihood point estimate — and \code{ci_method} is \code{"wald"}, or
\code{"boot"} (percentile bootstrap) for the unstandardized solution of
a fit with \code{se = "bootstrap"}. A fixed parameter (the marker
loading) has \code{p = NA}, as lavaan reports it. A \code{blavaan} fit is
refused: this method reports maximum-likelihood fits.

\item \code{apa_tidy(estimate_contrasts)}: A table of contrasts from
\code{\link[modelbased:estimate_contrasts]{modelbased::estimate_contrasts()}} on a Bayesian fit, as a \code{contrasts}
table. A row is named as modelbased names it, \code{Level1 - Level2}
(\code{"6 - 4"}, or \code{"6, auto - 4, auto"} for two variables), or by its
\code{Parameter} for a custom comparison; a \code{by} variable's values go into
the \code{group} column. The numbers are read from the table, not
recomputed, and so is the ROPE share with its bounds when the table
has one. The table records its interval only in its call: with no
\code{ci_method} there it is modelbased's default equal-tailed interval,
otherwise the one named. When the call gives \code{ci_method} as a
variable, or the table has lost its call, name the interval with
\code{ci = "eti"} or \code{ci = "hdi"}; a \code{ci} that contradicts the call is
refused. Tables computed with \code{backend = "emmeans"} are refused, since
their estimate column does not say which centrality it holds: pass
the emmeans grid to \code{apa_tidy()} instead.

\item \code{apa_tidy(estimate_means)}: A table of marginal means from
\code{\link[modelbased:estimate_means]{modelbased::estimate_means()}} on a Bayesian fit, as a \code{contrasts}
table whose rows are named by the values of their \code{by} variables
(\code{"4"}, \code{"4, auto"}). Everything else is read as for
\code{estimate_contrasts} tables.

\item \code{apa_tidy(parameters_model)}: A \code{parameters_model} table from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}}. A table of a Bayesian model
inherits \code{describe_posterior} and is handled by that method; a
table that does not — a frequentist model, or one computed with
several \code{ci} levels — is refused here rather than coerced to draws.

\item \code{apa_tidy(describe_posterior)}: A \code{describe_posterior} table from
\code{\link[bayestestR:describe_posterior]{bayestestR::describe_posterior()}}, or from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}} on a Bayesian model. The numbers
are read from the table, so \code{ci}, \code{ci_level}, \code{rope} and
\code{diagnostics} are not arguments: they were settled when the table
was computed, and \code{ci_method} and the interval level are read from
it. \code{centrality = NULL} reports whichever of the median and the mean
the table holds, and asks you to name one when it holds both.
\code{rhat} and \code{ess_tail} are read when present; \code{ess_bulk} is always
\code{NA}, because no easystats table carries it — pass the fit for both
ESS columns. \code{bf} is \code{exp(log_BF)} when the table was computed with
\code{test = "bf"}. The \code{package_versions} attribute records the
versions installed when \code{apa_tidy()} ran, not necessarily those that
computed the table.

\item \code{apa_tidy(stanreg)}: A \code{stanreg} object from rstanarm. Estimates,
interval, pd and the ROPE percentage come from
\code{\link[parameters:model_parameters]{parameters::model_parameters()}} with \code{priors = FALSE}; R-hat and
both ESS columns from \code{\link[posterior:summarise_draws]{posterior::summarise_draws()}} over the fit's
draws, which cover every reported parameter including \code{sigma}.
apabayes computes no summary of its own.

}}
\examples{
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("posterior")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
# Deterministic draws, so the example does not depend on RNG state:
# normal quantiles, reordered by a fixed rule so the chain is not
# monotone and the diagnostics are meaningful.
z <- stats::qnorm(stats::ppoints(400))
z <- z[order(sin(seq_along(z)))]
draws <- posterior::as_draws_df(
  data.frame(mu = 2 + z, sigma = exp(0.3 * z))
)
apa_tidy(draws)
apa_tidy(draws, variables = "mu", ci = "hdi", ci_level = 0.9)

# An easystats table you already computed is reported as it stands:
# the interval, its method and the ROPE are read off the object, not
# recomputed, so those are not arguments here.
apa_tidy(bayestestR::describe_posterior(draws))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("lavaan")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# A lavaan fit: a maximum-likelihood estimate with a Wald interval and
# a p value, so `centrality` is NA and `ci_method` is "wald".
fit <- lavaan::cfa(
  "visual =~ x1 + x2 + x3",
  data = lavaan::HolzingerSwineford1939
)
apa_tidy(fit, component = "loading", standardize = TRUE)
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("brms")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# `brms::hypothesis()` reads a plain data frame of draws as well as a
# fitted model, so the hypothesis route needs no Stan here. The first
# row is directional (a 90\% interval, `bf10` the posterior odds), the
# second a point hypothesis whose evidence ratio needs prior draws
# this data frame does not carry.
q <- stats::qnorm(stats::ppoints(400))
draws <- data.frame(b_wt = -5 + q, b_am = 0.2 + 2 * q)
apa_tidy(brms::hypothesis(draws, c("b_wt < 0", "b_am = 0")))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("emmeans")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# An emmeans grid from a Bayesian fit carries the coefficient draws;
# `emmeans::qdrg()` builds one from a draws matrix directly, which is
# what a brms or rstanarm fit would supply. The interval is the HDI,
# emmeans's own HPD interval; `ci = "eti"` asks for the equal-tailed one.
q <- stats::qnorm(stats::ppoints(400))
coefs <- cbind(
  `(Intercept)` = 26.7 + q, cyl_f6 = -6.9 + 1.5 * q,
  cyl_f8 = -11.6 + 1.3 * q
)
cars <- transform(mtcars, cyl_f = factor(cyl))
grid <- emmeans::qdrg(~cyl_f, data = cars, mcmc = coefs)
means <- emmeans::emmeans(grid, ~cyl_f)
apa_tidy(means)
apa_tidy(emmeans::contrast(means, "pairwise"), rope = c(-1, 1))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed(c("modelbased", "rstanarm", "marginaleffects", "collapse"))) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# Four packages are guarded for, not one: `estimate_contrasts()` does
# its work through marginaleffects, a Suggests of modelbased rather
# than a dependency, and marginaleffects does this path's through
# collapse. Measured on CI, one run apart. The version modelbased
# asks of marginaleffects is in `DESCRIPTION`.
# A modelbased table is reported as it stands. It records its interval
# only in its call: none named there is modelbased's equal-tailed
# default, and a variable there has to be named with `ci =`.
\donttest{
cars <- transform(mtcars, cyl_f = factor(cyl))
fit <- rstanarm::stan_glm(
  mpg ~ wt + cyl_f,
  data = cars, chains = 2, iter = 1000, seed = 1, refresh = 0
)
apa_tidy(modelbased::estimate_contrasts(fit, contrast = "cyl_f"))
method <- "hdi"
means <- modelbased::estimate_means(fit, by = "cyl_f", ci_method = method)
apa_tidy(means, ci = "hdi")
}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed(c("correlation", "BayesFactor"))) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# Bayesian correlations. The table records neither its interval nor
# its centrality, so `ci` and `centrality` name them; the defaults are
# correlation's own.
r <- correlation::correlation(
  mtcars[, c("mpg", "wt", "hp")],
  bayesian = TRUE
)
apa_tidy(r)
r_eti <- correlation::correlation(
  mtcars[, c("mpg", "wt")],
  bayesian = TRUE, bayesian_ci_method = "eti"
)
apa_tidy(r_eti, ci = "eti")
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("BayesFactor")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}

# A BayesFactor object is read as a table of model comparisons, with
# BayesFactor's own names, prior family and numerical error; its
# inclusion Bayes factors come from bayestestR; its estimates from the
# draws of `BayesFactor::posterior()`.
cars <- transform(mtcars, am_f = factor(am), vs_f = factor(vs))
bf <- BayesFactor::anovaBF(mpg ~ am_f * vs_f, data = cars, progress = FALSE)
apa_tidy(bf)
apa_tidy(bayestestR::bayesfactor_inclusion(bf))
bf_t <- BayesFactor::ttestBF(formula = mpg ~ am_f, data = cars)
apa_tidy(BayesFactor::posterior(bf_t, iterations = 500, progress = FALSE))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=apabayes_tidy]{apabayes_tidy()}} for the contract.
}
